<node id="ArXiv">
  <data key="d0">ArXiv</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">ArXiv is a preprint repository where several cited papers were published.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5f0a5a74b5a4eac43772c74649ca651</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A knowledge graph constructed by RAG-Anything where non-text atomic units such as images, tables, and equations are transformed into structured graph entities to preserve modality-specific semantic information.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph grounds non-textual modalities within their contextual environment, containing parent and child nodes for multimodal instances.&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-modal knowledge graph is built from the extracted entities and relations and is later fused with a text-based knowledge graph through entity alignment.&lt;SEP&gt;The cross-modal knowledge graph is a unified graph structure representing multimodal knowledge, constructed by RAG-Anything and later merged with a text-based knowledge graph through entity alignment.&lt;SEP&gt;A knowledge graph constructed by RAG-Anything that preserves modality-specific grounding by linking entities extracted from textual descriptions to anchor nodes representing non-textual content.&lt;SEP&gt;A knowledge graph that is fused with the text-based knowledge graph via entity alignment to form the unified knowledge graph G.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-885744df4898cd552f1960ba1b518cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238e7dae37f77eecdb2fe491a026dc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808bf767f815e787e09b6b3e8a122b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Text-Based Knowledge Graph">
  <data key="d0">Text-Based Knowledge Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A traditional knowledge graph constructed from text modality chunks using named entity recognition and relation extraction techniques, similar to LightRAG and GraphRAG methodologies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Text-Based Knowledge Graph is constructed using established text-centric extraction pipelines and contains entities such as Bce, Beecher, and Chemistry.&lt;SEP&gt;The text-based knowledge graph is fused with the cross-modal knowledge graph through entity alignment to form a unified knowledge representation.&lt;SEP&gt;The text-based knowledge graph is a graph that is merged with the cross-modal knowledge graph through entity alignment.&lt;SEP&gt;A knowledge graph that is merged with the edge set $\tilde{E}$ through entity alignment to form the unified graph.&lt;SEP&gt;A knowledge graph that is fused with the cross-modal knowledge graph via entity alignment to form the unified knowledge graph G.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-885744df4898cd552f1960ba1b518cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238e7dae37f77eecdb2fe491a026dc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808bf767f815e787e09b6b3e8a122b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Knowledge Graph">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Knowledge Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A graph structure built using non-text units as anchor points, where each atomic non-text unit is associated with a multimodal entity node serving as an anchor for its intra-chunk entities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514180</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entity Alignment">
  <data key="d0">Entity Alignment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A process that merges the multimodal knowledge graph and text-based knowledge graph using entity names as primary matching keys to identify semantically equivalent entities across both structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Entity Alignment is the process through which complementary cross-modal and text-based knowledge graphs are merged.&lt;SEP&gt;Entity alignment is the process through which the cross-modal knowledge graph is fused with the text-based knowledge graph to form a unified knowledge representation.&lt;SEP&gt;Entity alignment is the process by which the cross-modal knowledge graph is merged with a text-based knowledge graph.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which the edge set $\tilde{E}$ is merged with the text-based knowledge graph to form the unified graph.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which the cross-modal knowledge graph and the text-based knowledge graph are fused to form the unified knowledge graph G.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-885744df4898cd552f1960ba1b518cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238e7dae37f77eecdb2fe491a026dc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-808bf767f815e787e09b6b3e8a122b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Graph Fusion">
  <data key="d0">Graph Fusion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The integration of cross-modal and text-based knowledge graphs into a unified knowledge representation that captures both multimodal contextual relationships and text-based semantic connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph fusion is a subsequent stage that integrates the constructed graphs into a unified structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514447</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dense Representation Generation">
  <data key="d0">Dense Representation Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A process that constructs a comprehensive embedding table encoding dense representations for all graph entities, relationships, and atomic content chunks across modalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A step in RAG-Anything's indexing pipeline that the equation is central to, producing dense vector representations of knowledge components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-885744df4898cd552f1960ba1b518cb4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514428</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Large Language Models">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Large Language Models</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Models leveraged by RAG-Anything to derive complementary textual representations from each atomic content unit, including detailed descriptions and entity summaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Atomic Units">
  <data key="d0">Atomic Units</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Decomposed units abstracted from diverse file formats that preserve both content and structural context, maintaining hierarchical order and contextual relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Entity Node">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Entity Node</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A node associated with each atomic non-text unit that serves as an anchor for its intra-chunk entities through explicit belongs_to edges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Named Entity Recognition">
  <data key="d0">Named Entity Recognition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique used in the text-based knowledge graph extraction process to identify entities within textual content.&lt;SEP&gt;Named entity recognition is a text-based pipeline technique that the extraction routine R(·) is analogous to for identifying entities.&lt;SEP&gt;Named Entity Recognition is a task whose final label generation sequence is the subject of the question in the failure case, involving characters, embeddings, LSTM layers, and a CRF layer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37ff528cd8f38cd79fa57c96fab4a7c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514439</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Relation Extraction">
  <data key="d0">Relation Extraction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique used in the text-based knowledge graph extraction process to identify semantic relationships between entities.&lt;SEP&gt;Relation extraction is a text-based pipeline technique that the extraction routine R(·) is analogous to for identifying relations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Graph Extraction Routine">
  <data key="d0">Graph Extraction Routine</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A routine denoted as R(·) that processes the description of each non-text unit to identify fine-grained entities and relations.&lt;SEP&gt;The graph extraction routine R(·) is a procedure that extracts intra-chunk entity sets from the textual descriptions of non-text units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intra-Chunk Entities">
  <data key="d0">Intra-Chunk Entities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sets of entities identified within each atomic non-text unit, denoted as ν_j, along with their relations E_j.&lt;SEP&gt;Intra-chunk entities are the set 𝒱_j of entities extracted from a non-text content unit's description.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Belongs_To Edges">
  <data key="d0">Belongs_To Edges</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Explicit edges that associate intra-chunk entities with their corresponding multimodal entity node anchor.&lt;SEP&gt;Belongs_to edges are the connections that anchor extracted entities and relations to the multimodal entity node v_j^{mm}.&lt;SEP&gt;Explicit edges that anchor each intra-chunk entity to the multimodal entity node associated with the same non-text unit, preserving modality-specific grounding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0238e7dae37f77eecdb2fe491a026dc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contextual Window">
  <data key="d0">Contextual Window</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter denoted as δ that controls the contextual window size when processing each unit with its local neighborhood.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Detailed Description">
  <data key="d0">Detailed Description</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A textual representation denoted as d_j^chunk derived from each atomic content unit, optimized for cross-modal retrieval.&lt;SEP&gt;Detailed description is a JSON key whose value lists guidelines for analyzing an image, including describing composition, identifying objects, explaining relationships, noting colors and style, describing actions, including technical details, referencing surrounding content, and using specific names instead of pronouns.&lt;SEP&gt;Detailed Description is a JSON key in the Table Analysis Prompt response that lists analytical requirements such as table structure and organization, column headers and their meanings, key data points and patterns, statistical insights and trends, relationships between data elements, significance of the data in relation to surrounding context, and how the table supports or illustrates concepts from the surrounding content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eea2cc84733111d4a7fedb10d7083b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-840e794a234c66ff0680c138d02d19ef</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514427</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entity Summary">
  <data key="d0">Entity Summary</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A textual representation denoted as e_i^entity containing key attributes such as entity name, type, and description for graph construction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eac0008c777dab7291aec281277e77a1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Knowledge Unification Decomposition (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Knowledge Unification Decomposition (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (1) defines the decomposition of a knowledge source $k_i$ into atomic content units $c_j=(t_j,x_j)$, where $t_j$ is the modality type and $x_j$ is the raw content. This formalizes the Multimodal Knowledge Unification process in RAG-Anything, enabling unified representation and retrieval of heterogeneous multimodal content.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0efdf5167592c14e899f82452298f2ca</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="RAG-Anything Framework Architecture (image)">
  <data key="d0">RAG-Anything Framework Architecture (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This diagram illustrates the architecture of the RAG-Anything framework, a universal RAG system for multimodal knowledge. It details the pipeline from input documents through multimodal knowledge unification, dual-graph construction (combining text-based and cross-modal graphs), and vector database creation, culminating in a hybrid retrieval process that feeds into an LLM for response generation. The figure visually supports the paper's description of handling heterogeneous data types like text, images, tables, and equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-808bf767f815e787e09b6b3e8a122b71</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Modal Graph Extraction Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Modal Graph Extraction Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the extraction of intra-chunk entities and relations from the detailed description of a non-text atomic unit, enabling the construction of a cross-modal knowledge graph in the RAG-Anything framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-93e73b1153bf931b315a02bf890fff6d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph Vertex Set (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph Vertex Set (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the vertex set \tilde{V} of the cross-modal knowledge graph as the union of multimodal anchor nodes v_j^{mm} and intra-chunk entities \mathcal{V}_j extracted from non-text unit descriptions. This set-theoretic construction grounds non-textual content in a graph structure, enabling cross-modal retrieval and reasoning within the RAG-Anything framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-960c77c28320f020a2006dba07f70f59</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph Edge Set Construction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph Edge Set Construction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the edge set $\tilde{E}$ of the cross-modal knowledge graph as the union of intra-chunk relations $\mathcal{E}_j$ and 'belongs_to' edges linking each intra-chunk entity to its multimodal anchor node $v_j^{\mathrm{mm}}$. This preserves modality-specific grounding and contextualizes non-textual content within its textual neighborhood, supporting cross-modal retrieval and reasoning in RAG-Anything.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0238e7dae37f77eecdb2fe491a026dc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Dense Embedding Table Construction (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Dense Embedding Table Construction (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the embedding table τ as the set of dense representations emb(s) for all entities, relations, and content chunks in the unified knowledge graph. It enables efficient similarity-based retrieval by mapping all multimodal components into a shared vector space, forming the retrieval index T = (G, τ) for cross-modal hybrid retrieval.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-885744df4898cd552f1960ba1b518cb4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Statistics of Experimental Datasets (DocBench and MMLongBench) (table)">
  <data key="d0">Statistics of Experimental Datasets (DocBench and MMLongBench) (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 1 compares DocBench and MMLongBench benchmarks used to evaluate RAG-Anything. DocBench has 229 documents (avg. 66 pages, 46,377 tokens, 5 types, 1,102 questions); MMLongBench has 135 documents (avg. 47.5 pages, 21,214 tokens, 7 types, 1,082 questions). The table quantifies the benchmarks' scale and diversity, supporting the text's claim of comprehensive evaluation for long-context multimodal document understanding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5eb0987c79c221cedef1a930b87f821f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Multimodal Response Generation Equation (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multimodal Response Generation Equation (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the final response as the output of a Vision-Language Model conditioned on the query, structured textual context, and dereferenced visual content, integrating retrieved multimodal knowledge for grounded synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e906fa59bd4a41ccc46a928ffee1aa2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Accuracy Comparison (table)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Accuracy Comparison (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 2 compares accuracy of GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAGAnything on DocBench across five domains and three document types. RAGAnything achieves the highest overall accuracy (63.4%) and excels in Finance, News, and Multimodal types, supporting the paper's claims of superior cross-domain generalization and multimodal understanding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49961962320a3499cad5000e71cf7f3e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Domain-Specific Accuracy Comparison (table)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Domain-Specific Accuracy Comparison (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 3 compares accuracy of GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAGAnything across seven domains on MMLongBench. RAGAnything achieves the best overall accuracy (42.8%) and leads in five domains, particularly Research Reports (46.6%) and Financial Reports (43.6%). This supports the paper's claim that RAG-Anything's multimodal graph-based approach excels in long-context, information-dense domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8b8b33a1f15bc5b31bb493efd3ec933a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Accuracy vs. Page Range Comparison (image)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Accuracy vs. Page Range Comparison (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This line graph compares the accuracy of RAGAnything and MMGraphRAG on the DocBench benchmark across various document lengths. It illustrates that while performance is comparable for shorter documents, RAGAnything significantly outperforms MMGraphRAG as document length increases, particularly for documents over 100 pages. This visual evidence supports the paper's claim that RAG-Anything's dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval are highly effective for long-document reasoning tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d1f82685779db44d104d20c4e16fe8b7</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench QA Counts by Page Range (image)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench QA Counts by Page Range (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Bar chart showing the number of QA pairs in DocBench across five page ranges: 1-10, 11-50, 51-100, 101-200, and 200+. The highest counts are in the 1-10 and 11-50 ranges (around 340-350), while longer documents have significantly fewer QA pairs (down to about 80 for 200+ pages). This distribution contextualizes the performance gains of RAG-Anything on longer documents, as discussed in the surrounding text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8cb795e1c89d1938a0fcb44cbec5be74</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Accuracy Comparison by Page Range (image)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Accuracy Comparison by Page Range (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Line chart comparing RAGAnything and MMGraphRAG accuracy on MMLongBench across page ranges. RAGAnything consistently outperforms, with the gap widening for longer documents, supporting the paper's claim of superior long-context performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-162b82c942fb3baf527c7b1adf1ed920</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="QA Pair Count by Page Range Bar Chart (image)">
  <data key="d0">QA Pair Count by Page Range Bar Chart (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Bar chart showing the distribution of QA pairs across document length categories (1-10, 11-50, 51-100, 101-200, 200+ pages). The highest count is in the 11-50 page range, with a sharp decline for longer documents. This figure supports the performance comparison analysis in the RAG-Anything paper, illustrating the dataset composition for evaluating long-context performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-301da515d50a3a0d178c0683bee24465</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Ablation Study Results on DocBench (table)">
  <data key="d0">Ablation Study Results on DocBench (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table presents an ablation study on the DocBench dataset, comparing the full RAG-Anything model against two variants: 'Chunk-only' and 'w/o Reranker'. The results show that the full model achieves the highest overall accuracy (63.4%), validating the importance of both graph construction and cross-modal reranking. The 'Chunk-only' variant performs worst (60.0%), highlighting the critical role of the dual-graph architecture in capturing structural and cross-modal relationships for multimodal document reasoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21f9c375189978b339552ff8ff4ba819</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-panel Figure Interpretation Case Study (image)">
  <data key="d0">Multi-panel Figure Interpretation Case Study (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3 from the RAG-Anything paper illustrates a case study where the system correctly answers a question about a multi-panel t-SNE figure, while baseline models (GPT-4o-mini, MMGraphRAG, LightRAG) fail. The image shows the source document, evidence figure, and model responses, highlighting RAG-Anything's ability to focus on the correct panel (style space) and avoid confusion from adjacent panels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3ef854a849174a61602e3f9d4a8eac1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Financial Table Navigation Case Study (image)">
  <data key="d0">Financial Table Navigation Case Study (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4 from the RAG-Anything paper illustrates a financial table navigation case. It shows a query about Novo Nordisk's 2020 wages and salaries, an evidence table with the correct value (26,778 million DKK) highlighted, and compares answers from GPT-4o-mini, MMGraphRAG, LightRAG (all incorrect) against RAG-Anything (correct). The figure demonstrates RAG-Anything's ability to precisely navigate structured tables via graph-based retrieval, as discussed in the case study section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-17a576920c675eca791231767d46477b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Document Type Distribution and Statistics (table)">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Document Type Distribution and Statistics (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 5 presents document type distribution for the DocBench benchmark across five categories (Academic, Financial, Government, Legal, News). It reports the number of documents (40–50 per category), number of questions (148–303), and average pages per document (1–192). Financial documents are the longest (192 pages avg.), while News documents are the shortest (1 page avg.). Academic and Financial categories have the most questions. This table supports the surrounding text's description of DocBench as a diverse benchmark with varying document lengths and complexities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8cdd68412adb670ecbcf365fd5720250</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="MMLongBench Document Type Distribution and Statistics (Table 6) (table)">
  <data key="d0">MMLongBench Document Type Distribution and Statistics (Table 6) (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 6 reports document counts, question counts, and average page lengths across seven MMLongBench document types. Research reports lead in documents (34) and questions (292); Financial reports are longest (87 pages); Administrative files are shortest (17 pages). The data substantiates Appendix A.1's claim that MMLongBench spans diverse document types and lengths, providing exact figures for the stated 35–58 page moderate range, 78-page guidebooks, and compact brochures and administrative files.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86f8b3b086da6cca91c5573b8ff551ec</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="RAG-Anything Visual Reasoning Case Study (image)">
  <data key="d0">RAG-Anything Visual Reasoning Case Study (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This figure presents a visual reasoning case study comparing RAG-Anything's performance against baseline models (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG) on a chart interpretation task. The task requires identifying the GCAN sub-model component whose removal yields the lowest accuracy on the Twitter15 dataset. RAG-Anything correctly identifies the '-S-A' configuration by constructing a structured graph representation of the bar plot, while baselines misinterpret spatial relationships and provide incorrect answers. This demonstrates RAG-Anything's superior structure-aware multimodal understanding capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-306f65a0f4295fa49788ecf8659d4b81</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Tabular Navigation Case Study for RAG-Anything (image)">
  <data key="d0">Tabular Navigation Case Study for RAG-Anything (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6 presents a case study comparing RAG-Anything against baseline models (GPT-4o-mini, MMGraphRAG, LightRAG) on a tabular navigation task. The task requires identifying the model combination with the highest AUPRC value for the Evidence Inference dataset from a complex table with spatial ambiguity. RAG-Anything correctly identifies 'GloVe + LSTM - Attention' with a value of 0.506, while all baselines provide incorrect answers, demonstrating RAG-Anything's superior ability to handle structural ambiguity in tabular data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-89ff37f1683bc8edc5276effd3646dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Vision Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Vision Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of a code editor displaying a structured prompt template for vision analysis. The template defines a JSON output schema with fields for detailed description and entity info, and includes placeholders for context, image path, captions, and footnotes. This prompt is part of the RAG-Anything framework's context-aware multimodal prompting methodology, designed to guide models in extracting comprehensive visual descriptions aligned with surrounding text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840e794a234c66ff0680c138d02d19ef</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Table Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Table Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image shows the 'Table Analysis Prompt' template used in the RAG-Anything framework for structured tabular content decomposition. It instructs the model to analyze table structure, headers, data points, and relationships, and to output a JSON response with a detailed description and entity info. The prompt includes placeholders for context, image path, caption, body, and footnotes, aligning with the paper's description of systematic table analysis for enhanced retrieval accuracy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7eea2cc84733111d4a7fedb10d7083b8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation Analysis Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Equation Analysis Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of a prompt template for analyzing mathematical equations, designed to elicit a JSON response with detailed mathematical interpretation and context. This template is part of a systematic approach to equation analysis within a multimodal RAG framework, as referenced in the surrounding content about context-aware prompting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d556fb722f8eac5fc92376e70d34e60</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Accuracy Evaluation Prompt Template (image)">
  <data key="d0">Accuracy Evaluation Prompt Template (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A screenshot of a text editor showing the 'Accuracy Evaluation Prompt' template used for assessing RAG system answers. The prompt defines a binary accuracy score (0 or 1) based on factual correctness, with instructions for handling partial matches, numerical values, lists, and unanswerable questions, and requires a JSON output with accuracy and reasoning. This figure (Figure 10) is referenced in the document as the standardized prompt for consistent factual assessment across question types, used with GPT-4o-mini in the experimental framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b4b25ee421d57dc99861b0d26ac02b54</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Modal Misalignment Failure Case in Multimodal RAG (image)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Modal Misalignment Failure Case in Multimodal RAG (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image illustrates a failure case in multimodal RAG systems where all evaluated models (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, RAG-Anything) incorrectly answer a question about a neural network sequence. The expected answer requires reading a figure from bottom to top, but models exhibit text-centric bias and rigid spatial processing, producing incorrect sequences. This demonstrates fundamental challenges in cross-modal information integration and structural reasoning, as discussed in the surrounding text on challenges and future directions for multimodal RAG.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-37ff528cd8f38cd79fa57c96fab4a7c2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-modal Noise Case Failure Analysis (image)">
  <data key="d0">Cross-modal Noise Case Failure Analysis (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 11 illustrates a failure case in multimodal RAG where all methods incorrectly answer a question about model style separation. The evidence table is irregular, with merged cells and unclear boundaries, causing retrieval bias toward noisy textual evidence. The responses from GPT-4o-mini, MMGraphRAG, LightRAG, and RAG-Anything all provide incorrect answers, highlighting challenges in cross-modal alignment and structural reasoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a908e853be8da852064e031c68c78242</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514315</data>
</node>
<node id="DocBench Dataset">
  <data key="d0">DocBench Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The DocBench dataset is a benchmark used to evaluate accuracy of methods across various domains and document types.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49961962320a3499cad5000e71cf7f3e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514424</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RAGAnything">
  <data key="d0">RAGAnything</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">RAGAnything is a method evaluated on the DocBench dataset, achieving the highest overall accuracy of 63.4% and excelling in Finance, News, and Multimodal categories.&lt;SEP&gt;RAGAnything is a method evaluated on MMLongBench that achieves the highest overall accuracy of 42.8% and leads in five of seven domains.&lt;SEP&gt;RAGAnything is the name used in the DocBench Accuracy graph to represent the RAG-Anything framework, showing its accuracy across different document page ranges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49961962320a3499cad5000e71cf7f3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1f82685779db44d104d20c4e16fe8b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8b33a1f15bc5b31bb493efd3ec933a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/data/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791514425</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Documents">
  <data key="d0">Legal Documents</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal Documents is a domain category in the DocBench dataset evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal documents are a document category in DocBench containing 46 documents, 191 questions, and averaging 58 pages per document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49961962320a3499cad5000e71cf7f3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cdd68412adb670ecbcf365fd5720250</data>
